== A Use of Reproof

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[A use of reproof of all unmerciful men. God has laid many charges on us to be rich in good works, ready to give, willing to share, to draw out our souls to the hungry, to open our hearts to the needy, and he has given us many encouragements to this in his word; and yet these men shut up their hearts of compassion against the poor, refusing to give them any help or relief.] Surely such men have no love for Christ. For who can say that he loves the Lord Jesus Christ in truth and sincerity, when he allows his ministers and members to go without necessities, even food and clothing? Is this your love for Christ, to let him starve? It is plain that you love your money more than Christ, and so you come under that fearful #emph[anathema] in 1 Cor 16:22: “If anyone does not love the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be #emph[Anathema Maranatha.]” These two words, though both denote a curse, are from two different languages. The first word, #emph[Anathema,] is a #emph[Greek] word meaning #emph[accursed,] and it is used so in Rom 9:3 and Gal 1:8. The second is a #emph[Syriac] word, as #emph[Beza] observes in his annotations on 1 Cor 16:22, yet often used by the #emph[Jews.] It means #emph[our Lord comes: Maran] is “our Lord,” and #emph[atha] is “comes.” It implies that those who were under this curse were to expect no mercy, but to look for the dreadful coming of Christ to take vengeance on them. So, as St. #emph[James] says in ch. 2:13, “Judgment without mercy will be shown to anyone who has not been merciful.” How do they think to find mercy from Christ, who never showed mercy to him and his?]

I will not condemn all for unmercifulness (God forbid that I should). There are many (and I wish to God there were more) who contribute bountifully to the needs both of ministers and of other poor saints of God. But I still have too much cause to say that the greatest part of the rich men among us have their hearts hardened and their compassion shut up, so that they cannot feel for the wants of the poor; and their hands are withered, like the hand of the man in the Gospel, so that they cannot stretch them out to any good use.

Oh, that such men would consider that unmercifulness is a greater sin than they imagine! It was one of #emph[Sodom]’s sins, which brought down fire and brimstone from heaven on them and all their children (Ezek 16:49). Uncharitable men are cursed in their life, and so at their death; but they will be most cursed at the day of judgment. Being barren, fruitless trees in God’s orchard, they will surely be cut down with the barren fig tree and thrown into unquenchable fire.

To show you more particularly the miseries of uncharitable persons:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[They are accursed here in everything: all they have is cursed.] So much is implied in that saying of our Savior, Luke 11:41: “Give as alms such things as you#super[+] have, and everything is clean for you#super[+].” But without almsgiving nothing is clean for you#super[+]; that is, nothing is blessed and sanctified to you#super[+], but all things are defiled and cursed. Not only are your#super[+] crosses curses, but your#super[+] blessings are curses to you#super[+]. So God threatens #emph[to curse their very blessings] (Mal 2:2). Though you enjoy an abundance of this world’s goods, yet as long as you are hardhearted to the poor and needy, the curse of God clings to your store and abundance. Oh, how sad and lamentable your condition must be, when those things that are not only blessings in themselves, but blessed to others as well, are cursed to you! Surely, to be accursed like this is misery enough.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[As the unmerciful are cursed here, so they will be cursed hereafter.] Jas 2:13: “Judgment without mercy will be shown to anyone who has not been merciful.” Those who have shut up their compassion against the needs of the poor, God will shut up his compassion against; he will let out his fury on them, and they will have their portion in his plagues and indignation, without the least drop of pity or mercy. And at the day of judgment Christ will say to them, Matt 25:41: “Depart from Me, you#super[+] who are cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.” There is their doom. And why? “For I was hungry and you#super[+] gave Me nothing to eat, I was thirsty and you#super[+] gave Me nothing to drink.” Oh, dreadful sentence! Every word of it carries great terror in it and breathes nothing but fire and brimstone. What! Must they #emph[depart from Christ,] the fountain of bliss and happiness? And into #emph[eternal fire?] Ah, wretches! Cursed indeed. For as the prophet says in Isa 33:14, “Who of us can dwell with a consuming fire? Who of us can dwell with everlasting burnings?” An everlasting fever, or even an everlasting toothache, would be an unspeakable misery. But what are these compared with #emph[lying in that lake which burns with fire and brimstone to all eternity?] Oh, I think the very name of #emph[eternal fire] and #emph[everlasting burnings] should awaken all uncharitable men out of their careless security, and stir them up without any further delay to mourn their past unmercifulness, to beg pardon for it, and sincerely to resolve to give willingly out of their store toward the relief of the poor, in proportion to what God has graciously given them. It may be that you have a plentiful portion of this world’s goods. But oh, what will it profit you to live plentifully here, and to be eternally miserable hereafter? I truly cannot help standing amazed when I consider how men can believe the words of Christ to be true, that all uncharitable men will be sentenced by him at the last day to everlasting burning for not feeding the hungry and not clothing the naked, and yet can be so hardhearted as not to listen to the cry of the poor.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. #emph[The misery of uncharitable persons appears in this: the wants and needs of the poor cry loudly to heaven against them.] God has #emph[dealt bountifully with you, loading you with his benefits] (as the psalmist says, Ps 68:19). He has given you not only food and clothing, things needful and necessary, but an abundance, a plenty of outward things, even all things richly to enjoy. But how many of Christ’s ministers and members are in great want, without the means to satisfy their own and their children’s hunger? Their miseries, like the blood of #emph[Abel,] cry to God for vengeance against you, saying, “Lord, there are many who have enough and to spare, a generous portion of this world’s goods; like #emph[Dives] they feast sumptuously every day. But what are we, Your servants in want, the better for them? Which of us is warmed by their fires, or clothed with their garments, or even shares in the crumbs from their table? We are ready to perish for want, while they are glutted with their abundance. Is the blessing of those who are ready to perish likely to come upon them? Will You not judge them, O Lord?” Certainly these bitter and pitiful complaints cry loudly in the ears of the Almighty against such unchristian and inhuman misers. Beware of the cry of the poor against you#super[+]. If you will not hear their cries to you, God will hear their cries against you.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Your unmercifulness to the poor will provoke God to reject your most religious exercises. Prov 21:13: “Whoever shuts his ears to the cry of the poor, he too shall cry out and receive no answer.” #emph[In these words] Solomon #emph[hints to us two things.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. That unmerciful men, who turn their ears away from the cry of the poor, will fall into such miseries as will bring them to their knees and make them cry out, sooner or later. Here in this world, worldly men sometimes say many prayers, and they hope their religion will make amends, at least for their inhumanity. And in the world to come they will follow their fellow #emph[Dives] in his devotions in hell.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Though they cry, yet they will not be heard.] Whether they cry here on their deathbeds for mercy, or hereafter in hell for relief, they will not be heard. We have a notable instance of this in #emph[Dives.] Though he did not cry here, yet he cried in hell, saying, “Father Abraham, have mercy on me and send Lazarus to dip the tip of his finger in water and cool my tongue. For I am in agony in this fire” (Luke 16:24). But was he heard? Was his request granted? It was only a small request, and yet it is denied. Because he denied crumbs of bread to the poor here in this life, he was denied a drop of water in hell.#footnote[The 1676 printing adds here: #emph[Non habuit guttam, quia non dedit micam.] Aust. (“He did not have a drop, because he did not give a crumb”: Augustine.)]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as your prayers are rejected by God, so too it will be in vain for you to hear the word or to come to the table of the Lord. Shall I say that all your religion will be in vain? No, it will be an abomination to the Lord, as long as that accursed thing, your covetous and cruel heart, which the Lord abhors (Ps 10:3), remains within you. Do not think that one duty will excuse the neglect of another: that your praying may serve instead of your alms, that religion will excuse your lack of mercy, or that your confessing your unmercifulness will make up for it. The Lord abhors you and all your sacrifices as long as the sacrifice of a compassionate heart is missing.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Oh, that all uncharitable persons would steep their thoughts in a serious meditation on these things! And as they desire to escape the judgments that follow all merciless men, I wish they would put in for a share in the mercies of the merciful. To that end, they would put on hearts of pity and compassion toward the wants and miseries of God’s distressed ones, and stretch out a helping hand toward their relief. This leads me to a second use, namely,]]
